      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "Mei investigates an enzyme that breaks down a substrate. She measures the initial rate of product formation under seven conditions. Each trial uses the same amount of enzyme and the same reaction volume. The inhibitor concentration, when present, is identical across trials. The results below are simulated experimental data.\n\n| Condition | Temperature (°C) | pH | Substrate concentration (mM) | Inhibitor present | Product formation (µmol/min) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n| A | 25 | 7 | 1 | No | 40 |\n| B | 37 | 7 | 1 | No | 80 |\n| C | 65 | 7 | 1 | No | 4 |\n| D | 37 | 4 | 1 | No | 12 |\n| E | 37 | 7 | 1 | Yes | 20 |\n| F | 37 | 7 | 10 | No | 100 |\n| G | 37 | 7 | 10 | Yes | 90 |\n\nIn a separate recovery test, Mei exposes enzyme samples to either 65°C at pH 7 or 37°C at pH 4 for five minutes without substrate. She then returns both samples to 37°C and pH 7 and adds substrate at 1 mM. The heat-treated sample produces product at 4 µmol/min, while the acid-treated sample produces product at 80 µmol/min.",
      "question": "Respond to parts (a)–(d), using the experimental results to support your explanations.\n\n(a) Identify the condition that serves as the appropriate control for determining the inhibitor's effect in condition E. Calculate the percent decrease in reaction rate caused by the inhibitor under these conditions. Show your calculation.\n\n(b) Explain the increase in reaction rate from condition A to condition B and the decrease from condition B to condition C. Include molecular motion and enzyme structure in your response.\n\n(c) Explain how the change in pH could produce the result in condition D. Explain what the recovery test suggests about the difference between the effects of acidic conditions and high temperature on this enzyme.\n\n(d) Identify whether the results more strongly support competitive inhibition or inhibition that cannot be overcome by increasing substrate concentration. Justify your answer using both substrate concentrations, and explain a molecular mechanism consistent with the results.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Identifies the appropriate control",
          "description": "Identifies condition B because it matches condition E in temperature, pH, and substrate concentration but lacks the inhibitor.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Calculates the percent decrease",
          "description": "Shows a calculation equivalent to ((80 − 20) / 80) × 100 and obtains a 75% decrease.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Explains the increase from 25°C to 37°C",
          "description": "Explains that increased molecular motion increases the frequency of productive enzyme–substrate encounters, increasing the reaction rate while the enzyme retains a functional structure.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Explains the decrease at 65°C",
          "description": "Explains that high temperature can disrupt interactions maintaining enzyme structure, altering the active site and reducing substrate binding or catalysis.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Explains the effect of acidic pH",
          "description": "Explains that altered pH changes the protonation or charges of enzyme groups, which can disrupt structural interactions or active-site function and reduce activity.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Interprets the recovery test",
          "description": "Uses recovery to 80 µmol/min after acid treatment and persistence at 4 µmol/min after heat treatment to explain that the acidic effect was reversible under the tested conditions, whereas heat caused a lasting loss of functional enzyme structure under those conditions.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Identifies and supports the inhibition model",
          "description": "Identifies competitive inhibition as more strongly supported and compares inhibitor-treated rates with the corresponding controls: 20 versus 80 µmol/min at 1 mM substrate and 90 versus 100 µmol/min at 10 mM substrate.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Explains a consistent molecular mechanism",
          "description": "Explains that substrate and inhibitor compete for the active site, so increasing substrate concentration increases substrate occupancy and reduces the inhibitor's effect. Does not require the inhibitor to permanently alter enzyme shape.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "Condition B isolates the inhibitor's effect in condition E; the inhibitor decreases the rate by 75%.",
        "Moderate warming increases productive molecular encounters, while sufficiently high temperature can disrupt enzyme structure and active-site function.",
        "Acidic pH can alter charges and interactions within the enzyme; the recovery results distinguish reversible pH effects from lasting heat damage under the tested conditions.",
        "The inhibitor's smaller relative effect at higher substrate concentration supports competition between substrate and inhibitor for the active site."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) Choose B because only inhibitor presence differs from E. Calculate ((80 − 20) / 80) × 100 = 75%.\n(b) From 25°C to 37°C, faster molecular motion increases productive encounters. At 65°C, disruption of structural interactions can alter the active site, sharply lowering activity.\n(c) At pH 4, changes in protonation can interfere with structural interactions or catalytic groups. Recovery to 80 µmol/min suggests that the acidic effect is reversible. Failure of the heated sample to recover suggests lasting structural damage under the tested conditions.\n(d) Competitive inhibition is more strongly supported. Increasing substrate reduces inhibition from 75% to 10% relative to the matching controls. More substrate can outcompete an inhibitor for active-site occupancy; these data support this mechanism without proving it uniquely."
    }
  ]
}

